    "result_text": "Done. The test passes and the front-end typechecks cleanly.\n\n## What I implemented\n\nA full-stack \"delete a project, keeping a record\" feature, added as new files following the app's additive conventions (no existing files edited except adding one query helper).\n\n**Server (OfficeFloor REST):**\n- `rest/api/projects/{projectId}/delete.POST.yml` \u2014 new endpoint wiring\n- `ProjectDelete.java` \u2014 looks up the project (404 if unknown), deletes it, and routes the audited side-effect through the `Audit` service, appending `PROJECT_DELETED id=<id>` \u2014 the record the spec reads from the audit file\n\n**Front-end (additive, per CLAUDE.md rules):**\n- `features/projects/queries.ts` \u2014 added `deleteProject(id)` (POST, shared `['projects']` key)\n- `features/projects/delete.slot.tsx` \u2014 a new `*.slot.tsx` filling the existing `project.row` region with a `data-testid=\"project-delete-<id>\"` button; it's a `useMutation` that `invalidateQueries(['projects'])` on success, so the row disappears from every view showing projects for free\n\n**No DB migration** \u2014 deleting rows needs no schema change, and the \"record that I did\" requirement is satisfied by the audit file (the app's declared side-effect channel), not a new table.\n\nThe test in `e2e/specs/acceptance.spec.ts` passes: after clicking `project-delete-1`, the row is gone, the list drops to one, and `PROJECT_DELETED id=1` is in the audit log.",
